{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OPERATOR DECISION (2026-08-07): the fallback chain stays OFF until this ticket lands — AND this ticket is committed to, not deferred.\n\nThe operator challenged my framing of the options, correctly. I had offered 'leave it off' and 'fix first, then enable' as separate choices; they are the same choice, differing only in whether this ticket is scheduled. There is no argument for leaving the underlying defect unfixed, so it is scheduled.\n\nThe defect, restated so it stands alone: when two models are configured as a chain, the runner INTERSECTS capabilities across candidates (runner.py:441). us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6 is measured native-structured-output-capable (ticket 18ae) while its direct-Anthropic twin deliberately stays PromptedOutput. So configuring the chain silently moves EVERY standard-class call project-wide from native to prompted structured output — a measured decision undone as a side effect of an unrelated resilience setting. test_provider_parity.py catches it and was deliberately left catching it rather than relaxed.\n\nWorth fixing regardless of whether the Anthropic fallback is ever enabled: capability intersection means ANY future chain — a second Bedrock region, an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, a cheaper model for a low-stakes class — pays the same silent tax. The resilience feature is currently unusable without a capability regression, which is a defect in the CHAIN MECHANISM, not in the choice of fallback provider.\n\nDirection for whoever implements: the up-front intersection is the wrong default. A chain should not degrade the PRIMARY's capabilities to match a fallback that may never be invoked; capability should be resolved per-attempt against the model actually being called. The real design question — answer it explicitly rather than by intersecting defensively — is what happens when a fallback attempt cannot honour a request shape the primary already accepted.\n\nRelated, and NOT resolved by this ticket: the direct-Anthropic arm is currently unreachable in non-interactive contexts for a separate reason — a valid key exists in ~/.zshrc but an INVALID one shadows it in the process environment agents and gates run in. See the credential-shadowing diagnosis recorded on 2876 (interactive key -> HTTP 200, non-interactive -> HTTP 401, different hashes, same length). Enabling the chain before that is fixed would exercise an invalid credential."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786115029680784001,"uuid":"87b605f3-9245-4a1b-be19-9c49fa544f35"}